      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:8332cd3b66b2324290e8dec50ad08ebff3fd7c7ff11a2949a5398cc54c13ddcd",
      "text": "What published sources report about occupational therapy, external cueing and gait training in Parkinson's disease. NICE guideline NG71 and an AOTA practice guideline address occupational therapy. The OTiP trial reported a favourable primary outcome, the PD REHAB trial reported no difference on its primary outcome, and a Cochrane review found insufficient evidence. The APTA guideline rates external cueing and gait training as high evidence quality with strong recommendation strength, yet meta-analyses differ on the best cue modality, and a 2026 meta-analysis found no significant reduction in freezing-of-gait severity. Treadmill meta-analyses report improved walking speed but not cadence. Authors describe cueing durability as unclear and wearable cueing as evaluated mainly in laboratories. This is an attributed summary of sources, not medical advice.",
